# Named domain literals

Domain / protocol string values must be declared as named constants and referenced by those identifiers — do not inline the same literal across the codebase.

## Requirements

- Closed sets and repeated protocol values (CLI args, upgrade modes, strategies, report formats, alignment actions, etc.) live as named constants.
- Prefer a single source of truth next to the type (e.g. `src/config/types.ts`), using `as const` objects so the type is derived from the constants.
- Comparisons, defaults, CLI parsing, and report metadata must use the identifiers (`UpgradeModes.SameMajor`), not `"same-major"`.
- When editing a file that still inlines such a literal, introduce or reuse the constant in the same change.

```typescript
export const UpgradeModes = {
  SameMajor: "same-major",
  Latest: "latest",
} as const;
export type UpgradeMode = (typeof UpgradeModes)[keyof typeof UpgradeModes];
```

## Does not require

- One-off literals with no domain meaning (error message prose, log text, paths already covered by helpers)
- External CLI argv that is not our protocol (e.g. git’s `worktree` subcommand)
- Package version / lockfile fixture strings in tests that are sample dependency data
- The **definition site** of the constant itself (the one place the string may appear)

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — inlined protocol literals
if (name === "inplace") { /* ... */ }
const strategy = args.strategy ?? "worktree";

// ✅ GOOD — named constants
if (name === StageStrategies.Inplace) { /* ... */ }
const strategy = args.strategy ?? StageStrategies.Worktree;
```
